14460:
For the reversible reaction:
\text{A} + \text{B} \rightleftharpoons \text{C} + \text{D}
The equilibrium constant expression is:
K_{\text{eq}} = \frac{[\text{C}][\text{D}]}{[\text{A}][\text{B}]}
Substituting the given equilibrium concentrations:
[\text{A}] = 2\text{ mol L}^{-1}, [\text{B}] = 3\text{ mol L}^{-1}, [\text{C}] = 10\text{ mol L}^{-1}, [\text{D}] = 6\text{ mol L}^{-1}
K_{\text{eq}} = \frac{10 \times 6}{2 \times 3} = \frac{60}{6} = 10
The standard Gibbs free energy change is given by:
\Delta G^\circ = -2.303 R T \log_{10} K_{\text{eq}}
Given R = 2\text{ cal mol}^{-1}\text{ K}^{-1} and T = 300\text{ K}:
\Delta G^\circ = -2.303 \times 2 \times 300 \times \log_{10}(10)
\Delta G^\circ = -1381.80 \times 1 = -1381.80\text{ cal}

Thus, the correct option is (D).
→ (D)
